5 #edb/ref 17592186410296 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410410 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410410 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410370 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410410 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410410 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410410 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186410296 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410410 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c3ced3f9-cf87-5626-9fe8-981ef8efa9ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410430 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410396 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410430 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143441" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410430 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410430 #edb/ref 1071 "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu =4$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=9,$  determine the smallest sample size required so that the standard error of the mean is less than $0.3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410430 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8503a82e-cf30-5876-b2c2-50de2ef2f695" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410430 #edb/ref 1074 "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nSince the population standard deviation is $\\sigma =\\sqrt{9}=3,$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe require that the standard error is less than $0.3.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{n}} & <0.3 \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\frac{3}{0.3} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >10 \\\\ n & >{10}^{2} \\\\ n & >100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest sample size is $n=101.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410438 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410410 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410438 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94453" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410438 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410438 #edb/ref 1071 "A data set has mean $\\overset{―}{x}=17$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=4.06.$ Find the $z$-score of an observation $x=12.3,$ giving your answer to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410438 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "eb3ce7a3-f936-5690-b0b8-a114f6f8251b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410438 #edb/ref 1074 "The $z$-score of an observation $x$ is defined by\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma }.\n$$\n\nThe $z$-score represents how many standard deviations an observation is above or below the data set's mean.\n\nHere, we're told that the mean is $\\overset{―}{x}=17,$ and we can calculate the standard deviation as\n\n$$\n\\sigma =\\sqrt{{\\sigma }^{2}}=\\sqrt{4.06}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $z$-score of the observation $x=12.3$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma } \\\\  & ≈\\frac{12.3-17}{\\sqrt{4.06}} \\\\  & ≈-2.33\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
